# Feasibility Analysis & Implementation Plan: Local Image Storage

## Feasibility Report

**Will this strategy work properly for your project?**
**YES. This is an excellent and highly feasible strategy.** 

Here is why your proposed architecture perfectly aligns with the current MERN setup:

1.  **Memory Storage + Sharp:** You are already using `multer.memoryStorage()` in `Backend/src/middleware/upload.js` and `sharp` in `Backend/src/services/cloudinary.service.js`. Routing the `req.file.buffer` through Sharp and then `fs.promises.writeFile()` is cleaner and faster than using `multer.diskStorage()`, avoiding temporary disk writes.
2.  **Backward Compatibility:** The database just stores URLs as strings. By returning `https://domain.com/uploads/images/filename.webp`, the database requires absolutely zero changes.
3.  **Frontend (`OptimizedImage.jsx`) Compatibility:** Your frontend component `Frontend/src/modules/Food/components/OptimizedImage.jsx` already checks `isCloudinaryUrl(src)`. If it detects a Cloudinary URL, it runs its custom optimizations. If it detects a standard URL (which your new local URLs will be), it will simply fall back to requesting the standard image URL (with ignored query parameters). **It will not break.**
4.  **Delete Logic:** By parsing the URL/publicId in the deletion flow, we can seamlessly branch the logic: local URLs get `fs.unlink()` and Cloudinary URLs get `cloudinary.uploader.destroy()`. Old uploads will be deleted cleanly from Cloudinary, and new ones from your VPS.

